#c114[i4] The hcs_source tool validates that the requested path resides inside the server's root using insideRoot; it throws "file not found" if the file is absent, and determines the excerpt range via symbol lookup or explicit start/end, defaulting to the whole file.
  > src/mcp.ts:198-212 #198f568e3ce7
#c115[i3] The rangeOf helper returns null when the underlying file cannot be read, otherwise computes a line range based on relocated paths, symbols, or explicit start/end, defaulting to the full file when no bounds are given.
  > src/mcp.ts::rangeOf #b27d384c1294
#c116[i3] scoreClaim builds a lowercase haystack string from the claim text, optional section, and each anchor's path and optional symbol.
  > src/mcp.ts::scoreClaim #deb2b38225db
#c117[i3] scoreClaim splits the haystack on any non‑alphanumeric or underscore characters, discarding empty tokens, to produce a list of words.
  > src/mcp.ts::scoreClaim #deb2b38225db
#c118[i3] scoreClaim returns the sum of terms for which at least one word in the haystack starts with the term.
  > src/mcp.ts::scoreClaim #deb2b38225db
#c119[i5] serveStdio creates an HcsMcpServer using the resolved file path and, if provided, the resolved root directory.
  > src/mcp.ts::serveStdio #d92f15dce5a4
#c120[i5] serveStdio writes a readiness message to stderr, ensuring logs are not mixed with protocol output.
  > src/mcp.ts::serveStdio #d92f15dce5a4
#c121[i5] serveStdio reads stdin line‑by‑line, ignoring lines that are empty or whitespace only.
  > src/mcp.ts::serveStdio #d92f15dce5a4
#c122[i5] If a line cannot be parsed as JSON, serveStdio writes a JSON‑RPC parse error (code -32700) to stdout and continues processing.
  > src/mcp.ts::serveStdio #d92f15dce5a4
#c123[i5] For each successfully parsed request, serveStdio forwards it to server.handle; if the handler returns a response, it is serialized and written to stdout, otherwise no output is produced.
  > src/mcp.ts::serveStdio #d92f15dce5a4
#c124[i5] The module converts an .hcs document into a compact context block that an LLM reads.
  > src/render.ts:1 #21e972c5e23a
#c125[i5] RenderOptions lets callers control claim status handling, anchor inclusion (default true), token budget, and whether to keep stale or suspect claims.
  > src/render.ts::RenderOptions #da5202237ba2
#c126[i4] importance() returns a claim's priority from an iN tag, defaults to 5 for manual claims and 3 otherwise.
  > src/render.ts:28-35 #bc96d0d1701f
#c127[i5] selectClaims filters out stale or missing claims unless keepStale is true, and excludes suspect or high‑risk claims unless keepSuspect is true.
  > src/render.ts::selectClaims #c510bd10896d
#c128[i5] When a budget is set, selectClaims ranks eligible claims by importance then original order, estimates token cost, and keeps a claim only if cumulative cost stays within the budget.
  > src/render.ts::selectClaims #c510bd10896d
#c129[i3] claimLine formats a claim as "id: text" and optionally appends formatted anchors and an "(unverified)" suffix for stale or missing claims.
  > src/render.ts::claimLine #c7708f4778f1
#c130[i4] renderContext outputs an <hcs-context> block, escapes double quotes in the title, inserts section headers when the section changes, and marks stale/missing claims with "(unverified)".
  > src/render.ts::renderContext #31ee6bf0bfa6
#c131[i3] contextDigest returns a hash of the rendered context, optionally respecting a token‑budget limit.
  > src/render.ts:84-87 #1ea4f7fd61d6
#c132[i5] The module exports a `Verdict` type limited to the four string literals "correct", "partial", "incorrect", and "unknown".
  > src/score.ts::Verdict #312aa6bf5d46
#c133[i5] `ProbeResult` records the raw answer, the first‑run verdict, how that verdict was reached ("match", "judge", or "abstain"), all verdicts across runs, and the mean points.
  > src/score.ts::ProbeResult #1650e753ea3b
#c134[i5] `ScoreSummary` reports overall fidelity (mean points), a 95 % bootstrap confidence interval, shares of misled and abstained answers, probe and run counts, detailed results, and optionally judge‑agreement statistics.
  > src/score.ts::ScoreSummary #0bb744a7bb01
#c135[i5] `answer` sends a prompt to the LLM that forces it to answer only from the supplied context and requires the exact response "UNKNOWN" when it cannot answer.
  > src/score.ts::answer #1f2769f469c7
#c136[i5] `scoreContext` grades each probe (optionally multiple runs), aggregates verdicts, computes per‑probe points, overall fidelity, confidence intervals, misled/abstained shares, and when two judges are used also calculates agreement and Cohen's kappa.
  > src/score.ts::scoreContext #2bd796d2fbfb
#c137[i4] `judgesOf` returns the configured judge LLMs, defaults to the answerer when none are supplied, and throws an error if more than two judges are provided.
  > src/score.ts::judgesOf #d01411649f69
#c138[i5] compareContexts scores two contexts (or null) with scoreContext, computes per‑probe point differences, and returns a Comparison object containing average difference, 95% CI, and counts of better, worse and same probes.
